00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:34.000
So first off, let's start
with an introduction

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.740
to AutoML Vision.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:40.540
And to start with that, we have
the mission for the computer

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:42.230
vision group as a whole.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:45.370
And that's to help developers
build the next generation

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:48.680
computer vision-powered
autonomy solutions.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:53.950
And so this blue bar over here
represents the area of work

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:57.820
that we're focused on, that
being our pretrained machine

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.040
learning APIs and AutoML.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.440
AutoML being-- there
are three products

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:07.090
that launched at Google Next
2018 just a few months ago.

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:10.480
That would be AutoML Vision,
AutoML Natural Language,

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.400
and AutoML Translation.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.730
And the foundation for
those ML APIs and AutoML

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:19.000
are the technologies
and research

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:23.770
and the various
decades of work that

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:28.840
have happened on the Google
Machine Perception team.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:34.180
The investments into TensorFlow
and Cloud-hosted TPUs

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:38.350
drive these sort of development
of these products, which

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:42.100
can then in turn drive a
variety of different use cases.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:48.640
And those use cases span a
large variety or a large gamut

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:52.390
ranging from energy, like
say, like in the vision case,

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:57.220
like being able to detect spots
of corrosion on a wind turbine,

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:59.680
for example, all the way
to something like retail

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:01.930
where maybe you have
a product catalog

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:04.720
and you want to recognize
objects in photos

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.060
that match that catalog.

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:11.200
So as it turns out, the
pretrained machine learning

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:15.580
API, the Vision
API, can actually

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.150
address a lot of
those use cases.

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:19.630
So it's a pretrained model.

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:22.720
It has a variety of
different features.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:26.710
And you can learn more about
it at cloud.google.com/vision.

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:32.890
But the variety of features
that it has are presented here.

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:36.610
These bottom four, on the bottom
right, the Handwriting OCR,

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:40.080
Product search, Object
localizer, and Web detection

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:44.620
were announced at Next
2018 back in July.

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:47.680
But there's a variety
of different use cases

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.520
that these features handle,
ranging from optical character

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:57.730
recognition, being able
to scan and transfer

00:05:57.730 --> 00:06:03.190
large amounts of paper documents
at scale, to labeling, to logo

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:07.030
detection, image properties
like common colors

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:08.830
or dominant colors.

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:12.670
So the pretrained Vision
API would handle a lot

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:14.080
of these particular cases.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:18.800
And if you go to the ML API
section on the Cloud website,

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.220
you'll see that there's
a Video Intelligence API.

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:24.700
There's a Natural Language
API, Translation API.

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:28.030
Translation API has been
around for a while, actually.

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:30.610
But the question
emerges is, what

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:33.020
happens if the data
that you're looking for

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:35.729
or the objective
that you're going for

00:06:35.729 --> 00:06:37.270
belongs to a different
domain and you

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:41.250
need to support these very
specific concepts that may not

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:44.410
necessarily be covered
by the Vision API?

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:49.247
And so probably the answer that
a data scientist might come up

00:06:49.247 --> 00:06:51.580
with is that they would create
their own computer vision

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:52.240
model.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:55.570
And so over here,
we have laid out

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:57.610
very roughly the
different stages

00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:00.340
of constructing that model.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:03.010
But along that way, there
is a variety of questions

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:04.990
that data scientists
would be asking.

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:08.170
And these are questions
that have come up

00:07:08.170 --> 00:07:12.070
in the UX research
and other initiatives

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:14.350
that we've been
doing in the past,

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:18.755
such as when you're doing
your data preprocessing, one

00:07:18.755 --> 00:07:20.380
of the questions you
might ask is well,

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:23.170
what preprocessing
techniques should I use?

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:27.190
When I move over to the next
couple of stages of the model

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:29.790
design and being able to tune
those parameters, the questions

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:32.350
that those users would
ask would be, well,

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:34.570
what techniques
should I be using?

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:36.700
If I'm doing deep-learning,
which architecture

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:38.950
should I be selecting and
what sort of hyperparameters

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:40.960
should I be tuning?

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:43.120
And then once you move
over to the evaluate step,

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:47.320
then it starts to get
into this whole goal

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:50.840
around being able to
analyze that data,

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:54.220
like understanding what metrics
are most important or most

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:55.690
relevant to my objective.

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:59.122
How do I know that my model's
actually performing well?

00:07:59.122 --> 00:08:01.330
What are some ways for me
to actually improve my data

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:02.740
set so that if I
were to retrain,

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:04.448
it would actually
produce better results?

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:10.450
And then for deployment, then
there is a question around,

00:08:10.450 --> 00:08:12.400
what infrastructure
should I be using

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:14.950
to be able to serve
this model at scale?

00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:19.530
Or how do I meet my
overall latency goals?

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:21.490
And then there's this
process of updating.

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:23.509
And then that cycles back
to the previous step.

00:08:23.509 --> 00:08:26.050
And so you might end up asking
these questions multiple times

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:31.610
as you're starting to go through
and improve your data set.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:34.450
And as you're answering these
questions along the way,

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:36.909
that could start to get
pretty expensive as you're

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:40.150
starting to choose all
these different pathways

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:43.059
and see what combinations work
best and produces the best

00:08:43.059 --> 00:08:45.370
outcome.

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:48.070
And then any time that you were
to, say, change your objective

00:08:48.070 --> 00:08:50.050
or shift it around,
then you have

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:55.340
to start over from the
beginning in many cases.

00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:57.740
So what we started to
look at with AutoML

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:00.880
is being able to take
all those complex steps

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:03.880
of building a ML model and being
able to automate it somehow.

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:06.730
And the way that
we'll show it is we'll

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:09.970
see how it manifests
in a user interface.

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:12.370
But what the goal
of AutoML at that

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:16.254
point is that it allows you to
focus on actually collecting

00:09:16.254 --> 00:09:18.670
the right data and being able
to define the right business

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:21.040
objectives and still
have a model that's

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:25.370
of high enough quality that you
can deploy it to production.

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:30.000
And so the workflow
that then manifests

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.674
gets distilled
down to, well, you

00:09:32.674 --> 00:09:34.840
put together your data set
with some training images

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:36.010
and labels.

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:38.050
You pass it into
AutoML, which will

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:40.750
do the whole process
of training your model,

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:43.960
deploying your model, and
serving your model on Google

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:45.150
Cloud.

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:48.190
And then what you end up with
is a simple REST interface

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:52.050
for making those
types of predictions.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:55.330
So what types of capabilities
does AutoML currently

00:09:55.330 --> 00:09:57.490
have or AutoML Vision
currently have?

00:09:57.490 --> 00:09:59.420
Well, we have single-label
classification.

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:04.090
So this is the case where you
might have an image that should

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:06.230
be only one of several classes.

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:07.960
So the example that
we bring up here

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:10.480
is one where you're trying to
distinguish different cloud

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:13.945
types from each other based on
the overall physical properties

00:10:13.945 --> 00:10:16.270
or visual properties
of these clouds.

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:19.120
And this could be
one of those inputs

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:22.427
to help build a pretty good
indication of what the weather

00:10:22.427 --> 00:10:23.260
is going to be like.

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:29.650
The other side of it would
be multilabel classification

00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:31.550
where an image can
have several classes.

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:33.310
So one example
might be maybe you

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:36.310
want to create this
giant latte art database.

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:38.590
And you have all these
different indicators

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:42.390
of what types of properties
this latte art should have.

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:44.587
And maybe at some point
you want to-- when

00:10:44.587 --> 00:10:46.420
people upload new images
of their latte art,

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:49.570
they can automatically
categorize and easily browsable

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:51.650
and all that.

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:54.640
But this would be a case where
you would not necessarily

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:57.610
want feather to win out
over latte or etching

00:10:57.610 --> 00:10:58.570
to win out over mocha.

00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:00.111
But rather, it's
like they would also

00:11:00.111 --> 00:11:02.320
share those common properties.

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:09.620
So what happens under the hood
is that AutoML can actually

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:11.220
use AI to build AI.

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:15.950
So what we mean by that is
that we actually introduce--

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:18.726
once you submit your
data set to AutoML,

00:11:18.726 --> 00:11:25.910
an AI agent will come in and be
able to build this model that

00:11:25.910 --> 00:11:28.550
uses deep learning
techniques that

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:31.640
has the optimal architecture and
set of hyperparameters to solve

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:34.100
your particular business need.

00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:37.650
And where that manifests,
like back in April,

00:11:37.650 --> 00:11:40.010
there was this
article that came out

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:43.880
that showed this Japanese
researcher that was

00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:46.040
able to take photos of ramen.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:48.740
And there's this shop
called Ramin Jiro.

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:51.320
They have 40-some locations.

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:56.330
And what this researcher was
able to do is take, I believe,

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:59.570
1,000 photos from
each of those shops

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:02.590
and then be able to
pass that into AutoML,

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:05.130
train it for about 24 hours.

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:06.850
And when all was
said and done, it

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:10.190
was able to predict which
specific shop that ramen came

00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:14.480
from with something like a 95%
accuracy, that being 94 point

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:17.030
something percent recall or
94 point something percent

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:18.050
precision.

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:20.750
So that was an
interesting case because I

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:22.520
myself an a giant ramen fan.

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:23.630
So I was kind of a--

00:12:23.630 --> 00:12:25.590
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:25.590 --> 00:12:28.300
It's very rare that articles
about AI make you salivate.

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:32.170
But it was-- that would
be one of a couple.

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:34.550
But anyway.

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:36.360
So where UX starts
to get involved

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:38.960
is this whole process
of creating user stories

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:40.250
and scenarios.

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:42.660
And so we'll start
with a simple one.

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:44.720
So we'll start with this user.

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:46.100
We'll call her Jane.

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:48.650
She's a developer at a
wildlife conservancy.

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:50.690
Maybe she's familiar
with Cloud Vision API.

00:12:50.690 --> 00:12:53.510
But she's probably familiar with
implementing APIs in general.

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:55.343
She happens to have a
lot of images on hand.

00:12:55.343 --> 00:12:59.780
But she has little
direct ML experience.

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:02.540
She's more of an ML enthusiast.

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:06.050
And so, unfortunately, this
photo is a little washed out.

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:08.660
But that's a little silhouette
of a flightless bird

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:09.660
right there.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:11.896
So what she wants to do is
create a bird classifier

00:13:11.896 --> 00:13:13.270
so she can improve
the experience

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:14.324
of her ornithology app.

00:13:14.324 --> 00:13:15.740
So maybe what ends
up happening is

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:20.180
she ends up using the Vision
API, the pretrained Vision API,

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:23.330
to handle a lot of her labeling
techniques or labeling tasks.

00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:26.300
But in the case of being
able to distinguish emus

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:29.540
from cassowaries, from
rheas to ostriches,

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:36.530
she wants to create an even more
custom model to address that.

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:40.200
And so what would be some
ways that she might do this?

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:43.500
Well, there's a couple
of different approaches.

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:46.190
One is that she could use
this Wizard of Oz approach,

00:13:46.190 --> 00:13:51.500
where when users submit
images, she'll surreptitiously

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:54.350
take these images and send
them to a human labeling pool

00:13:54.350 --> 00:13:58.022
somewhere that will go in and
label these images for her

00:13:58.022 --> 00:13:58.730
and send it back.

00:13:58.730 --> 00:14:01.200
And then this gets
indexed by the database.

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:03.710
But you can see
where this starts

00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:06.350
to get not all that scalable
in the long run, where

00:14:06.350 --> 00:14:09.170
the cost will increase
as the number of users

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:13.010
increases, the number of--
if she decides to expand

00:14:13.010 --> 00:14:15.620
her reach beyond just
these four classes, then

00:14:15.620 --> 00:14:18.140
that starts to complicate
how she interacts

00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:19.490
with this labeling service.

00:14:19.490 --> 00:14:23.050
And if she wants results
in a matter of seconds

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:25.220
rather than minutes
or days, then this

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:27.526
is probably not the
right solution for her.

00:14:27.526 --> 00:14:29.900
Another possibility is that
she could use the Vision API.

00:14:29.900 --> 00:14:31.649
But as mentioned
earlier, these categories

00:14:31.649 --> 00:14:33.680
may not necessarily be
all that specific enough

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:35.330
to meet her use case.

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:40.864
The third option, which we
brought up a few slides back,

00:14:40.864 --> 00:14:42.530
is that she could
write a model herself.

00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:44.630
But as mentioned by her
persona description,

00:14:44.630 --> 00:14:46.010
she doesn't have
the ML expertise

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:48.840
to do to build that model.

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:53.630
So what we offer as
the ultimate solution

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:56.450
is to use AutoML Vision,
where Google can basically

00:14:56.450 --> 00:15:00.020
train a model off of her
own images and labels.

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:02.090
And so this is where
we get into a demo.

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:05.060
And hopefully, this will work.

00:15:05.060 --> 00:15:10.160
So as a setup, I had created
a folder called birds

00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:10.740
on the left.

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:12.650
And within there, I
create four folders

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:14.307
with the different
labels that I want.

00:15:14.307 --> 00:15:15.890
And then within each
of those folders,

00:15:15.890 --> 00:15:19.130
I just threw in all
the images that I want

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:21.900
labeled ostrich or emu or rhea.

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:26.000
So if I can go here--

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:26.570
I'll go here.

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:29.660
And this is the AutoML
Vision interface.

00:15:29.660 --> 00:15:32.420
This is me creating
a new data set.

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:36.990
So I can go here and say,
OK, I'll call this birds.

00:15:36.990 --> 00:15:39.251
And from here, I can
import my images.

00:15:39.251 --> 00:15:40.750
And there's three
different options.

00:15:40.750 --> 00:15:46.410
So one option is the one that
is suited for my purpose, which

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:49.217
is to basically just upload
these images from my computer

00:15:49.217 --> 00:15:50.550
where I just select these files.

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:51.258
And I'll say, OK.

00:15:51.258 --> 00:15:53.560
I'll take this
birds [INAUDIBLE]..

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:55.980
And I'll pop in like so.

00:15:55.980 --> 00:15:57.690
The other main
option that we have

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:00.900
is that you can select a CSV
file on Google Cloud Storage.

00:16:00.900 --> 00:16:02.670
And this would be
particularly useful

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:06.750
if you have an inordinately
large amount of images,

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:09.900
in which case you might want
to use Google Cloud Storage's

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:14.045
tools in order to get
massive numbers of images

00:16:14.045 --> 00:16:15.445
into your data set.

00:16:15.445 --> 00:16:17.070
The other possibility
is that maybe you

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:20.490
want to control the
training validation testing

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:23.370
split, in which
case you can specify

00:16:23.370 --> 00:16:27.760
what bucket it should go into
within the CSV file itself.

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:30.180
And so there's a
little code over here

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:34.950
that describes how that
search syntax works, where

00:16:34.950 --> 00:16:41.670
you have the path to the
location of your image

00:16:41.670 --> 00:16:43.212
on Google Cloud Storage.

00:16:43.212 --> 00:16:44.670
And then you follow
it with a comma

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:46.840
and then put the label
or labels after it.

00:16:46.840 --> 00:16:49.920
And then if you wanted
to specify whether it's

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:51.420
in training,
validation, or testing,

00:16:51.420 --> 00:16:54.950
then you would prepend that
line with training, validation,

00:16:54.950 --> 00:16:55.830
or test.

00:16:55.830 --> 00:16:59.820
And so this is more for users
that are maybe a little bit

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:01.860
more advanced,
that would know how

00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:04.839
data splitting works and have
best practices around it.

00:17:04.839 --> 00:17:07.349
So we don't
necessarily surface it

00:17:07.349 --> 00:17:11.609
at this point, given that we
handle the split automatically

00:17:11.609 --> 00:17:14.700
if they just leave
it empty for now.

00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:16.290
And then the third
option is that they

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:18.250
can import their images later.

00:17:18.250 --> 00:17:20.589
So they essentially start
with an empty data set.

00:17:20.589 --> 00:17:23.369
And then they can, through
the interface itself,

00:17:23.369 --> 00:17:28.920
they can throw in their
images and label them--

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:31.920
label them manually
within the UI

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:34.410
or use the human
labeling service

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:36.552
that's also built into the UI.

00:17:36.552 --> 00:17:38.010
And then this last
option over here

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:40.260
is just basically
specifying what

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:41.700
the classification type is.

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:45.210
So it's single-label
classification by default.

00:17:45.210 --> 00:17:47.610
But you can optionally
choose to make

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:49.530
this multi-label classification.

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:51.480
So at this point, I would
hit Create Data Set.

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:55.030
And then it would churn
for some amount of time.

00:17:55.030 --> 00:17:56.940
It depends on the
size of your data set.

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:58.690
But I think for this
one, it took probably

00:17:58.690 --> 00:18:01.499
about 20 or 30 minutes or so.

00:18:01.499 --> 00:18:03.540
But we're not going to
wait 20 or 30 minutes here

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:05.164
because I don't really
have a whole lot

00:18:05.164 --> 00:18:07.090
to say during that time.

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:08.760
So what I'll end up
doing is I'll just

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:12.460
skip over to the actual
created data set here.

00:18:12.460 --> 00:18:13.960
So, hopefully, this works.

00:18:13.960 --> 00:18:16.650
So my internet connection has
been kind of wonky all day.

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:19.050
So we'll see.

00:18:19.050 --> 00:18:23.880
So here, this is after
I upload my zip file

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:26.010
through that Create Form.

00:18:26.010 --> 00:18:30.690
And 20 or 30 minutes later, it
pops in this browser over here.

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:34.080
And so I can see all the
images that were imported

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:37.270
and the labels that are
associated with them over here.

00:18:37.270 --> 00:18:39.300
And furthermore,
what I can do is

00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:42.000
I can narrow it down
to a specific label

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:44.460
here by clicking on
this item over here.

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:47.547
So I narrowed that to
just the cassowaries.

00:18:47.547 --> 00:18:48.380
They'd be over here.

00:18:48.380 --> 00:18:50.950
And I see my ostriches.

00:18:50.950 --> 00:18:53.370
And what I can
also do is I can go

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:56.190
in to individual images
over here and say, you know,

00:18:56.190 --> 00:18:59.365
this particular
image right here?

00:18:59.365 --> 00:19:01.490
Let's say, hypothetically,
that this wasn't an emu.

00:19:01.490 --> 00:19:02.970
It was actually an ostrich.

00:19:02.970 --> 00:19:05.970
I can go in here and say,
this is an ostrich, hit OK.

00:19:05.970 --> 00:19:08.260
And then it will
update that data set.

00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:13.510
So you can go in one by one
and updates these images,

00:19:13.510 --> 00:19:15.480
or you can select it over here.

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:18.300
And you can do a click--

00:19:18.300 --> 00:19:22.590
you can do the click select
or do a multiselect over here

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:26.910
to do multiple images,
and hit the label button

00:19:26.910 --> 00:19:32.440
and do a mass relabeling
of these images to say,

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:35.200
for example, emu.

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:36.790
So it's all of built in here.

00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:42.090
And it's also built to handle
a large list of labels as well.

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:44.850
So you have access to
filtering over here.

00:19:44.850 --> 00:19:48.420
So I can filter down to
cassowaries this way.

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:49.860
Or I can search select on here.

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:51.570
There's a filter over here.

00:19:51.570 --> 00:19:53.910
So it's geared
towards like being

00:19:53.910 --> 00:19:56.790
on the order of 100, a couple
hundred labels that you can

00:19:56.790 --> 00:19:59.760
potentially have in this case.

00:19:59.760 --> 00:20:03.580
Once I move over
to the Train tab,

00:20:03.580 --> 00:20:05.400
this would be the next step.

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:07.820
And at this point,
ordinarily I'd

00:20:07.820 --> 00:20:11.520
be prompted with a
product call to action

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:13.680
to allow me to
train a new model.

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:14.730
So I click on this guy.

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:17.460
And I can say, OK,
this is my model.

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:20.190
And I'll give it a one
compute hour training

00:20:20.190 --> 00:20:24.430
budget, which is free for the
first 10 models for each month.

00:20:24.430 --> 00:20:26.340
I also have the
option of specifying

00:20:26.340 --> 00:20:28.200
a larger number of
compute hours, which

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:30.330
we'll get to later.

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:34.120
And so once you submit
your training request,

00:20:34.120 --> 00:20:37.100
it'll churn for
another amount of time.

00:20:37.100 --> 00:20:40.550
And I think in this case, it
took about 15 to 20 minutes

00:20:40.550 --> 00:20:44.600
or so and produced a model
that looks something like this.

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:46.610
I actually trained two
models this morning.

00:20:46.610 --> 00:20:49.890
Actually, this one performed
slightly better than this one.

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:53.180
So the results that
I get is that I get

00:20:53.180 --> 00:20:55.730
an average precision of 0.947.

00:20:55.730 --> 00:20:59.450
And if I were one that
was not accustomed

00:20:59.450 --> 00:21:01.970
to these particular values,
I might look at this tool tip

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:05.510
and say, OK, what does
this 0.9 whatever mean?

00:21:05.510 --> 00:21:09.060
And so then if over here it says
that 1.0 is the maximum score,

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:12.560
so that means that's it's
looking pretty good right now.

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:15.480
My precision and recall
are good, but not great.

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:17.000
So maybe what I'll
do is I'll take

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:21.110
a look at the full evaluation
and see what's going on.

00:21:21.110 --> 00:21:24.440
And so clicking that See
Full Evaluation takes me

00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:27.600
to the third tab in this list,
which is the Evaluate tab.

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:31.670
And from here, I can take a
look at the All Labels panel

00:21:31.670 --> 00:21:36.190
and get a deeper dive into
these precision recall graphs.

00:21:36.190 --> 00:21:37.910
So I can move my
score threshold around

00:21:37.910 --> 00:21:43.340
and see how that affects the
precision recall graph as well

00:21:43.340 --> 00:21:46.830
as the precision at 1
and recall at 1 graph.

00:21:46.830 --> 00:21:50.030
So for a more
advanced ML user, they

00:21:50.030 --> 00:21:53.540
could see here what threshold
would make the best trade off

00:21:53.540 --> 00:21:56.300
between precision and recall.

00:21:56.300 --> 00:21:58.556
Or if they wanted to anchor
it more towards recall,

00:21:58.556 --> 00:21:59.930
they can move it
over here, which

00:21:59.930 --> 00:22:06.764
would produce more as false
positives as [INAUDIBLE]..

00:22:06.764 --> 00:22:08.430
And then if I were
to move it over here,

00:22:08.430 --> 00:22:12.230
then it would anchor it
more towards precision.

00:22:12.230 --> 00:22:15.050
An then down below here,
there's a confusion matrix.

00:22:15.050 --> 00:22:19.220
So this gives you a four
by four of the true labels

00:22:19.220 --> 00:22:21.320
versus the predicted labels.

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:23.930
And when I look
at this over here,

00:22:23.930 --> 00:22:26.700
I can see that for
cassowary and emu,

00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:28.580
it actually performed
pretty well.

00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:30.110
For ostrich, it performed OK.

00:22:30.110 --> 00:22:33.200
85% is good but not
particularly great.

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:36.120
But it really suffered
on the rhea class.

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:39.650
And so it looks like it confused
rhea with ostrich quite a bit.

00:22:39.650 --> 00:22:44.030
And having looked at birds
for the last couple of days--

00:22:44.030 --> 00:22:45.470
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:45.470 --> 00:22:49.370
--it seems that rheas, indeed,
