00:25:25.550 --> 00:25:30.680
Maybe it's something that I can
export this data set to a CSV

00:25:30.680 --> 00:25:32.210
file and look at this offline.

00:25:32.210 --> 00:25:35.070
There's a couple of different
ways to go about that.

00:25:35.070 --> 00:25:37.320
And then the last option
that you could potentially do

00:25:37.320 --> 00:25:41.710
is basically throw more
compute hours at this model.

00:25:41.710 --> 00:25:43.650
So we only trained it
with one compute hour,

00:25:43.650 --> 00:25:47.400
which actually translates to
about 15 to 30 minutes or so.

00:25:47.400 --> 00:25:50.250
But if I were to,
say, train a new model

00:25:50.250 --> 00:25:53.100
and give it a much more
substantial training budget,

00:25:53.100 --> 00:25:57.560
it's quite possible that
throwing more computer hours

00:25:57.560 --> 00:26:01.440
will give me a better
model with the same data.

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:04.200
But this is something
that you can max out on,

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:05.610
but you can also customize.

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:08.430
You can say, maybe I just
wanted to try out two at first

00:26:08.430 --> 00:26:10.260
and then see how things perform.

00:26:10.260 --> 00:26:12.510
And then I can go back
and resume training

00:26:12.510 --> 00:26:14.790
with an additional
set of compute hours.

00:26:14.790 --> 00:26:16.770
So there's a couple
of different options

00:26:16.770 --> 00:26:21.240
at my disposal as far as going
in and intervening and seeing

00:26:21.240 --> 00:26:24.330
if I can squeeze better
performance out of this model.

00:26:24.330 --> 00:26:27.090
And then the last step
that the user can do

00:26:27.090 --> 00:26:30.074
is go to this Predict section.

00:26:30.074 --> 00:26:31.740
This is sort of like
the moment of truth

00:26:31.740 --> 00:26:34.770
where they can click this
button and upload some hold out

00:26:34.770 --> 00:26:35.580
images.

00:26:35.580 --> 00:26:37.680
So I have this image over here.

00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:39.660
This appears to be an emu.

00:26:39.660 --> 00:26:41.710
So we'll see if this
thing will actually work.

00:26:41.710 --> 00:26:42.210
And, yeah.

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:48.030
It looks like it predicted
an emu with 0.996% score.

00:26:48.030 --> 00:26:50.850
Ostrich was slightly
represented,

00:26:50.850 --> 00:26:52.110
rhea slightly represented.

00:26:52.110 --> 00:26:56.160
But it's almost so infinitesimal
that it might not even

00:26:56.160 --> 00:26:57.070
matter all that much.

00:26:57.070 --> 00:26:58.903
And if I had set the
score threshold at 0.5,

00:26:58.903 --> 00:27:02.710
then this would be graded
as an emu, regardless.

00:27:02.710 --> 00:27:07.440
And then down below, we have
a couple of code samples

00:27:07.440 --> 00:27:10.530
that will allow you to
implement your custom model.

00:27:10.530 --> 00:27:13.980
So we provide some
snippets for the REST API.

00:27:13.980 --> 00:27:17.760
And you can toggle over here
to see a little mini Python

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:20.040
script so that you can--

00:27:20.040 --> 00:27:23.744
if you're a person
that happens to have

00:27:23.744 --> 00:27:26.160
a lot of training images on
hand but maybe not necessarily

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:28.560
a lot of programming experience,
you can take this guy

00:27:28.560 --> 00:27:31.500
and copy it and paste it and
send it to your web developer

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:33.330
and say, hey, can
you help out here

00:27:33.330 --> 00:27:36.280
and see if you can get
this thing to work?

00:27:36.280 --> 00:27:39.300
But in the meantime, the model
itself has been deployed to

00:27:39.300 --> 00:27:42.490
and is readily available
for predictions.

00:27:45.430 --> 00:27:50.830
So that's basically the long
and short of the workflow.

00:27:50.830 --> 00:27:53.240
And so let me just go
back to the presentation.

00:27:57.680 --> 00:28:01.390
And so from here,
we can conjure up

00:28:01.390 --> 00:28:03.580
some ideas on how all
this fits together,

00:28:03.580 --> 00:28:04.660
like different pieces.

00:28:04.660 --> 00:28:08.890
So unfortunately,
there's a boundary

00:28:08.890 --> 00:28:13.730
that circles these four
boxes in the center.

00:28:13.730 --> 00:28:17.150
But that represents the
Google Cloud Platform system.

00:28:17.150 --> 00:28:19.300
And so one workflow
that you can imagine

00:28:19.300 --> 00:28:23.380
is that maybe a user uploads
new images from their computer.

00:28:23.380 --> 00:28:26.200
It gets sent into
Google Cloud Platform.

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:28.270
It gets stored on Cloud
Storage, which will then

00:28:28.270 --> 00:28:32.020
trigger a new image notification
via the Pub/Sub Google Cloud

00:28:32.020 --> 00:28:36.540
product, which then gets sent
to App Engine, which is what

00:28:36.540 --> 00:28:38.710
the search index is based on.

00:28:38.710 --> 00:28:41.920
And what can happen there is
that, well, App Engine could

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:45.650
call the AutoML
Vision API and say,

00:28:45.650 --> 00:28:47.870
OK, I got these
three new images.

00:28:47.870 --> 00:28:49.390
What do these images look like?

00:28:49.390 --> 00:28:53.080
And the AutoML Vision API can
return back predictions on,

00:28:53.080 --> 00:28:56.800
well, this is an ostrich, this
is an emu, this is a rhea,

00:28:56.800 --> 00:28:59.560
and store that in a
search index on App Engine

00:28:59.560 --> 00:29:05.100
so that in step five, the user
can search for, say, ostrich

00:29:05.100 --> 00:29:08.409
or rhea and be able
to get those results

00:29:08.409 --> 00:29:09.950
automatically tagged
on their behalf.

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:16.190
Now let's take a step back
and take a deeper dive

00:29:16.190 --> 00:29:17.840
into the user experience.

00:29:20.990 --> 00:29:28.460
So user experience or UX is
in the most general terms,

00:29:28.460 --> 00:29:31.580
at least at Google, loosely
split into these three

00:29:31.580 --> 00:29:32.790
different branches.

00:29:32.790 --> 00:29:35.990
So there's UX research, UX
design, and UX prototyping.

00:29:35.990 --> 00:29:39.126
And you see that one
feeds into the other.

00:29:39.126 --> 00:29:41.000
And there's actually
some hidden arrows where

00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:44.060
it cycles back in on itself.

00:29:44.060 --> 00:29:46.910
So as like a primer
on what these three

00:29:46.910 --> 00:29:49.850
different disciplines are, so
UX research is all about better

00:29:49.850 --> 00:29:51.740
understand the problem space.

00:29:51.740 --> 00:29:55.340
And that could be through
interviews with stakeholders,

00:29:55.340 --> 00:29:58.400
with customers, doing
academic research,

00:29:58.400 --> 00:30:02.570
being able to gather insights
from a whole spectrum

00:30:02.570 --> 00:30:07.850
of sources, being able to
provide meaningful analysis

00:30:07.850 --> 00:30:12.500
and also interpret the results
so that it can be translated

00:30:12.500 --> 00:30:15.406
into actionable steps,
not just for UX design

00:30:15.406 --> 00:30:17.780
and prototyping but also for
the product management team,

00:30:17.780 --> 00:30:19.880
for engineering, et cetera.

00:30:19.880 --> 00:30:23.380
But as far as the scope
of this discussion,

00:30:23.380 --> 00:30:25.250
the primary artifacts
from UX research

00:30:25.250 --> 00:30:31.250
feed into UX design, which is
the area that I'm rooted in.

00:30:31.250 --> 00:30:33.480
And that's all
around constructing

00:30:33.480 --> 00:30:39.110
the flows, the overall
user journey, the way

00:30:39.110 --> 00:30:43.430
that the product is constructed
from a visual and information

00:30:43.430 --> 00:30:45.200
hierarchy standpoint.

00:30:45.200 --> 00:30:48.080
It can also encompass visual
design and motion design,

00:30:48.080 --> 00:30:51.770
so things like transitions or
web components are being used

00:30:51.770 --> 00:30:54.320
and how they look like,
the aesthetics are

00:30:54.320 --> 00:30:56.850
all part of that kind of realm.

00:30:56.850 --> 00:30:59.000
Then that feeds
into UX prototyping,

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:01.910
also known as UX
engineering at Google.

00:31:01.910 --> 00:31:06.230
And that is the ability to take
all these design and research

00:31:06.230 --> 00:31:13.220
artifacts and encapsulating it
into something that's tangible

00:31:13.220 --> 00:31:18.110
that we can show to
stakeholders, to other team

00:31:18.110 --> 00:31:19.320
members--

00:31:19.320 --> 00:31:21.860
be it as a spec or
as a demonstration--

00:31:21.860 --> 00:31:24.530
or most importantly,
in our case,

00:31:24.530 --> 00:31:27.140
to UX research participants.

00:31:27.140 --> 00:31:33.320
So this gives us an
early look at how

00:31:33.320 --> 00:31:36.500
the design is going
without necessarily getting

00:31:36.500 --> 00:31:40.310
too deep into the
implementation side of things.

00:31:40.310 --> 00:31:45.870
And the way that prototyping
manifests within the UX field

00:31:45.870 --> 00:31:48.900
is that it takes a variety of
different levels of fidelity.

00:31:48.900 --> 00:31:51.320
Like it can range from
very low fidelity,

00:31:51.320 --> 00:31:54.170
like just a series
of pieces of paper

00:31:54.170 --> 00:31:56.860
that have a bunch
of diagrams on it,

00:31:56.860 --> 00:32:01.310
that you just show in
sequence, kind of mixed around.

00:32:01.310 --> 00:32:03.320
It could be something
like a Google Slides

00:32:03.320 --> 00:32:06.030
deck or a PowerPoint deck where
it's like a series of mock ups.

00:32:06.030 --> 00:32:10.550
They take a participant through
or a stakeholder through.

00:32:10.550 --> 00:32:13.310
But then it can-- again, to
the very high fidelity where

00:32:13.310 --> 00:32:18.980
it's actually a semi-interactive
or fully interactive product

00:32:18.980 --> 00:32:24.440
that a participant can directly
interact with and see how

00:32:24.440 --> 00:32:28.110
does this meet
their expectations.

00:32:28.110 --> 00:32:30.740
And so one way
that this manifests

00:32:30.740 --> 00:32:33.320
is here on AutoML, where
this is like the live demo

00:32:33.320 --> 00:32:34.790
that I showed right here.

00:32:34.790 --> 00:32:37.820
This other tab right
here is the UX prototype

00:32:37.820 --> 00:32:40.700
that we've been building
over the last however long.

00:32:40.700 --> 00:32:44.580
And this is basically something
that is using not real data,

00:32:44.580 --> 00:32:50.330
but it's using as many UX
patterns and components

00:32:50.330 --> 00:32:56.530
put together to see how that
maps to users' expectations

00:32:56.530 --> 00:33:01.520
and allows us to get a head
start on how our design

00:33:01.520 --> 00:33:05.360
thinking is going without
leaping directly into bugging

00:33:05.360 --> 00:33:10.370
a bunch of engineers to make a
bunch of back end and front end

00:33:10.370 --> 00:33:15.070
modifications that may
end up not testing well.

00:33:15.070 --> 00:33:18.320
And so in this case, you
can see that there's--

00:33:18.320 --> 00:33:19.670
this is all fairly functional.

00:33:19.670 --> 00:33:22.100
You can go in and select
a bunch of images.

00:33:22.100 --> 00:33:26.060
You can assign some labels
and do all sorts of activities

00:33:26.060 --> 00:33:28.440
that you would normally
do with a live product.

00:33:28.440 --> 00:33:32.630
But this is all done all through
smoke and mirrors essentially.

00:33:32.630 --> 00:33:36.890
And it's basically more or less
like an inherently disposable

00:33:36.890 --> 00:33:41.180
tool that we can use to do
some rapid iterations on.

00:33:41.180 --> 00:33:43.702
So one example, which,
unfortunately, will not

00:33:43.702 --> 00:33:45.410
show up particularly
well on this screen,

00:33:45.410 --> 00:33:48.800
was with a confusion
matrix where we originally

00:33:48.800 --> 00:33:51.550
had started off
with a matrix that

00:33:51.550 --> 00:33:54.680
looks like this, where the
positive values or the matches

00:33:54.680 --> 00:33:55.610
were green.

00:33:55.610 --> 00:33:57.190
The negative ones were red.

00:33:57.190 --> 00:34:00.674
But a concern came up
that people with red/green

00:34:00.674 --> 00:34:02.090
colorblindness may
not necessarily

00:34:02.090 --> 00:34:05.990
see that diagonal strip of
green in the center there.

00:34:05.990 --> 00:34:10.010
It's actually looks all like
shades of yellow or brown.

00:34:10.010 --> 00:34:12.949
So what we ended up doing
was use this prototype

00:34:12.949 --> 00:34:16.639
as a means of being able to try
out a couple of different color

00:34:16.639 --> 00:34:18.289
palettes, so like
being able to see,

00:34:18.289 --> 00:34:20.830
oh, OK, let's see what it looks
like with a blue-gray themed.

00:34:20.830 --> 00:34:23.560
We ultimately settled on the
blue-orange theme over here

00:34:23.560 --> 00:34:26.260
because it gives a fair amount
of prominence between the two.

00:34:26.260 --> 00:34:29.287
But the ability
that we can toggle

00:34:29.287 --> 00:34:31.870
between these different variants
without necessarily impacting

00:34:31.870 --> 00:34:33.909
the rest of the flow
or having to crank out

00:34:33.909 --> 00:34:36.969
a bunch of additional mockups
was one of the key pieces

00:34:36.969 --> 00:34:38.679
that we got out of this one.

00:34:38.679 --> 00:34:43.060
Another quick example
is like looking

00:34:43.060 --> 00:34:44.570
at one of these images of here.

00:34:44.570 --> 00:34:47.059
You can see the
predictions on the right.

00:34:47.059 --> 00:34:49.100
But one thing that we
we're thinking about, well,

00:34:49.100 --> 00:34:51.433
what if we were to just tweak
the layout so that we want

00:34:51.433 --> 00:34:53.739
this column to appear on the
left-hand side as opposed

00:34:53.739 --> 00:34:57.940
to making a request through
to our friend engineers, like,

00:34:57.940 --> 00:34:59.830
try this out, see how it feels.

00:34:59.830 --> 00:35:01.750
We can just try it
out on the side,

00:35:01.750 --> 00:35:06.610
see where the pros and cons
of this particular layout--

00:35:06.610 --> 00:35:09.520
do things become more
readable, less readable?

00:35:09.520 --> 00:35:11.650
How does this align
with how they've

00:35:11.650 --> 00:35:14.650
interacted with previous
products in the Google

00:35:14.650 --> 00:35:15.360
Cloud, et cetera?

00:35:19.410 --> 00:35:22.620
So if I jump back
to the presentation

00:35:22.620 --> 00:35:26.950
here, so as we started to do
a series of these iterations,

00:35:26.950 --> 00:35:28.920
we had multiple
rounds of testing

00:35:28.920 --> 00:35:31.380
on the research side, which
led to a bunch of iterations

00:35:31.380 --> 00:35:33.630
on the design side, which
led to a bunch of iterations

00:35:33.630 --> 00:35:34.630
on the prototyping side.

00:35:37.800 --> 00:35:39.810
A series of UX schools
start to emerge.

00:35:39.810 --> 00:35:46.290
And I have a couple of examples
of where in the workflow

00:35:46.290 --> 00:35:48.040
these goals manifest.

00:35:48.040 --> 00:35:50.430
So the first one
was all around being

00:35:50.430 --> 00:35:53.910
able to ground the
users' mental model.

00:35:53.910 --> 00:35:59.040
And what that means
is that we end up

00:35:59.040 --> 00:36:04.560
taking the traditional
ML workflow, which, yes,

00:36:04.560 --> 00:36:07.920
is a semilinear process
but has this tendency

00:36:07.920 --> 00:36:10.890
to circle back in on
itself, where once you

00:36:10.890 --> 00:36:13.710
get to a certain point after,
say, evaluating your model,

00:36:13.710 --> 00:36:15.930
you may circle back and
label some more images

00:36:15.930 --> 00:36:18.280
and then go back through
this process again.

00:36:18.280 --> 00:36:20.400
But eventually, the
goal is to end up

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:22.800
at the very last step
over here and start

00:36:22.800 --> 00:36:32.460
to see what are the different
subtasks or user stories that

00:36:32.460 --> 00:36:36.000
align with these
different themes that are

00:36:36.000 --> 00:36:38.080
part of each of these steps.

00:36:38.080 --> 00:36:41.462
So it might be something that's
fairly straightforward like,

00:36:41.462 --> 00:36:43.920
well, as a user, I want to name
the data sets so that I can

00:36:43.920 --> 00:36:45.375
recall later, whereas
the user wants

00:36:45.375 --> 00:36:47.670
to specify whether this is
single-label classification

00:36:47.670 --> 00:36:50.250
or multilabel classification.

00:36:50.250 --> 00:36:51.980
On the import side to--

00:36:51.980 --> 00:36:53.580
on the other side,
as a user, I want

00:36:53.580 --> 00:36:56.220
to be able to access
code samples so

00:36:56.220 --> 00:37:01.590
that I can be able to implement
my model and deploy it.

00:37:01.590 --> 00:37:08.190
And where we start to see
some sort of consolidation

00:37:08.190 --> 00:37:12.810
was seeing if we can map these
different steps of the workflow

00:37:12.810 --> 00:37:16.540
to specific states within
the application itself.

00:37:16.540 --> 00:37:20.420
And so where that starts to
come to play is-- unfortunately,

00:37:20.420 --> 00:37:22.440
it's a little washed out, but--

00:37:22.440 --> 00:37:25.530
the idea that there is
a Create Form over here

00:37:25.530 --> 00:37:28.110
at the left, which
leads to this tab

00:37:28.110 --> 00:37:32.100
set over here that
encompasses these four steps.

00:37:32.100 --> 00:37:36.030
And you can toggle between
Images, Train Evaluate,

00:37:36.030 --> 00:37:38.610
and Test and Use, or predict.

00:37:38.610 --> 00:37:40.500
Because they're tabs,
you can navigate

00:37:40.500 --> 00:37:41.860
backwards and forwards.

00:37:41.860 --> 00:37:45.286
So here's-- maybe a
little bit easier to see,

00:37:45.286 --> 00:37:46.410
or there's the Create Form.

00:37:46.410 --> 00:37:50.040
And then over here, you see
the images on the top left--

00:37:50.040 --> 00:37:52.350
Train, Evaluate, and Predict--

00:37:52.350 --> 00:38:00.111
representing those
four stages of the ML

00:38:00.111 --> 00:38:04.180
flow that you can go back
in any particular order

00:38:04.180 --> 00:38:06.030
and jump forward in
any particular order.

00:38:06.030 --> 00:38:10.480
There's no requirement
to do all of the steps.

00:38:10.480 --> 00:38:12.270
But there's still
this forward momentum

00:38:12.270 --> 00:38:19.200
that propels you towards
this last step over here.

00:38:19.200 --> 00:38:21.840
Another piece that
came to the forefront

00:38:21.840 --> 00:38:24.810
was being able to educate
users on new concepts.

00:38:24.810 --> 00:38:27.600
And by "new," I
mean new to users

00:38:27.600 --> 00:38:30.150
that have little ML expertise.

00:38:30.150 --> 00:38:32.880
And so this would
be something that

00:38:32.880 --> 00:38:34.950
might be around
progressive disclosure,

00:38:34.950 --> 00:38:37.550
so like the tool tip that I
showed earlier where it's like,

00:38:37.550 --> 00:38:40.530
well, average precision is
this weird, foggy concept

00:38:40.530 --> 00:38:42.980
to people that might
not have ML expertise.

00:38:42.980 --> 00:38:45.270
And then precision recall,
they sound intuitive.

00:38:45.270 --> 00:38:47.550
But maybe we need to
provide some additional text

00:38:47.550 --> 00:38:52.212
to help bring more clarity
to this particular term.

00:38:52.212 --> 00:38:53.670
And so this is
something that we've

00:38:53.670 --> 00:38:56.070
been doing a couple of
subsequent iterations

00:38:56.070 --> 00:38:59.220
on to see if we can make
this language increasingly

00:38:59.220 --> 00:39:04.620
clear to people that might
struggle with understanding

00:39:04.620 --> 00:39:07.470
these broad concepts.

00:39:07.470 --> 00:39:09.600
And so it's something
that we're--

00:39:09.600 --> 00:39:13.200
it's still a work in progress.

00:39:13.200 --> 00:39:16.080
Additionally, things
like distinguishing

00:39:16.080 --> 00:39:19.020
between a false negative
and a false positive

00:39:19.020 --> 00:39:22.320
would be areas that we
would investigate more fully

00:39:22.320 --> 00:39:28.180
in the detailed view where we
showed the list of predictions.

00:39:28.180 --> 00:39:31.620
So here, we have a list
of labels and the scores

00:39:31.620 --> 00:39:32.832
they received.

00:39:32.832 --> 00:39:34.290
But then we also
indicate which one

00:39:34.290 --> 00:39:36.570
was the ground truth by
this little star over here

00:39:36.570 --> 00:39:41.610
and indicate, well, this label
because it was above 0.5--

00:39:41.610 --> 00:39:44.220
which was the score
threshold that you had set--

00:39:44.220 --> 00:39:45.990
and it was not the
ground truth, we're

00:39:45.990 --> 00:39:47.562
deeming it as a false positive.

00:39:47.562 --> 00:39:49.020
And then meanwhile,
this one that's

00:39:49.020 --> 00:39:51.600
below 0.5, that star
is false negative.

00:39:51.600 --> 00:39:55.350
So trying to reintroduce
these concepts over and over

00:39:55.350 --> 00:39:58.110
again through the product itself
is another thing that we're

00:39:58.110 --> 00:40:03.970
trying to do to produce a more--

00:40:03.970 --> 00:40:06.690
infuse more user education
directly into the UI.

00:40:06.690 --> 00:40:10.350
So that's AutoML Vision
more or less in a nutshell.

00:40:10.350 --> 00:40:12.750
And we have two
other products as

00:40:12.750 --> 00:40:15.210
mentioned earlier that
launched at Next as well.

00:40:15.210 --> 00:40:17.250
So we have AutoML
Natural Language,

00:40:17.250 --> 00:40:20.571
which is essentially like
the same principle as AutoML

00:40:20.571 --> 00:40:21.070
Vision.

00:40:21.070 --> 00:40:26.270
But it's dealing basically with
free form, unstructured text

00:40:26.270 --> 00:40:29.160
like documents.

00:40:29.160 --> 00:40:32.250
And the use cases
around that are--

00:40:32.250 --> 00:40:35.500
there's a variety of them
that are associated with that.

00:40:35.500 --> 00:40:37.920
And then down below, we
have AutoML Translation,

00:40:37.920 --> 00:40:40.440
which is more about being
able to take the existing

00:40:40.440 --> 00:40:43.380
Translation API and
being able to augment it

00:40:43.380 --> 00:40:46.050
with your specific use cases.

00:40:46.050 --> 00:40:51.870
So if there are certain needs
or certain words or idioms,

00:40:51.870 --> 00:40:56.820
pieces of vernacular that
the current Translation

00:40:56.820 --> 00:41:01.890
API is struggling with, if you
have a whole series of sample

00:41:01.890 --> 00:41:04.380
sentence pairs, you can pass
that into AutoML Translation

00:41:04.380 --> 00:41:07.270
and build a custom
model for that.

00:41:07.270 --> 00:41:09.390
So you can find out
more about AutoML

00:41:09.390 --> 00:41:12.790
by going to this link, which
is actually repeated over here.

00:41:12.790 --> 00:41:16.810
And this is also an opportunity
to promote our office hours.

00:41:16.810 --> 00:41:20.010
So if you have a lot of
questions around the underlying

00:41:20.010 --> 00:41:25.170
technology for AutoML or want to
find out more about how AutoML

00:41:25.170 --> 00:41:27.690
works under the
hood, it's possible

00:41:27.690 --> 00:41:29.580
that through office
hours, we can pair you up

00:41:29.580 --> 00:41:32.430
with somebody that might
have more of that knowledge

00:41:32.430 --> 00:41:33.291
base on there.

00:41:33.291 --> 00:41:33.790
All right.

00:41:33.790 --> 00:41:34.830
Well, thanks very much.

00:41:34.830 --> 00:41:36.930
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:36.930 --> 00:41:40.280
[MUSIC PLAYING]

